      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "In her letter to Descartes dated 16 May 1643, Princess Elisabeth formulated what remains the classic \"interaction problem\" for Cartesian substance dualism:\n> \"I ask you please to tell me how the soul of a human being (it being only a thinking substance) can determine the bodily spirits, in order to bring about voluntary actions. For it seems that all determination of movement occurs through the impulsion of the thing moved, according as it is pushed by that which moves it, or else it is dependent on the quality and shape of the surface of the latter. Contiguity is required for the first two conditions, and extension for the third. You completely exclude the latter from the notion you have of the soul, and the former seems to me incompatible with an immaterial thing.\"\n\nIn Cartesian mechanistic physics, physical causation operates strictly by collision and impulsion (*impulsio*), requiring spatial contact (*contactus*) between surfaces. Because Descartes defines *res cogitans* as completely non-extended (*non extensa*) and non-spatial, it has no surface, boundaries, or spatial coordinates. Therefore, by the very laws of Cartesian physics, it cannot physically push, strike, or direct the \"animal spirits\" (*esprits animaux*) in the pineal gland.",
