      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "Nagel’s landmark 1974 paper locates the fundamental crisis of physicalism not in technical gaps in neuroscience, but in the irreconcilable epistemological mismatch between physical objectivity and phenomenal subjectivity.\n\nNagel observes:\n> \"Conscious experience is a widespread phenomenon... fundamentally an organism has conscious mental states if and only if there is something that it is like to *be* that organism—something it is like *for* the organism.\"\n\nIn **Assertion (A)**, Nagel chooses bats because they are mammals (phylogenetically related to us) that possess a sensory apparatus radically alien to ours: echolocation / sonar. Even if a human neuroscientist maps every single acoustic nerve fiber, sonic reflection, and brain region in the bat, the human can at best imagine what it would be like *for a human to behave as a bat*, not what it is like *for the bat itself*.\n\nIn **Reason (R)**, Nagel reveals the deep conceptual reason: The entire progress of physical science consists in reducing subjectivity by abstracting away from human sensory vantage points toward objective, observer-neutral formulations (e.g., understanding lightning not as visual flash, but as electrical discharge). However, when applying this method to consciousness itself, the method collapses:\n> \"If the subjective character of experience is fully comprehensible only from one point of view, then any shift to greater objectivity—that is, less attachment to a specific viewpoint—does not take us closer to the real nature of the phenomenon: it takes us farther away from it.\"\n\nThus, (R) provides the definitive theoretical rationale for (A).",
