      "question": "In \"Minds, Brains, and Programs\" (1980), John Searle argues against \"Strong AI\" using the Chinese Room thought experiment. When critics proposed the **Systems Reply**—asserting that while the man locked in the room does not understand Chinese, the *whole system* (consisting of the man, the rule book, the paper slips, and the room) does understand Chinese—how did Searle refute this reply?",
