      "thinkers": "Paul M. Churchland (1981)",
      "question": "In his landmark paper \"Eliminative Materialism and the Propositional Attitudes\" (1981), Paul Churchland argues that our common-sense conceptual framework of the mind—known as **Folk Psychology** (which explains human behavior in terms of *beliefs*, *desires*, *intentions*, and *fears*)—is not an innate truth, but an empirical, explanatory theory that is fundamentally false. \n\nConsider the following arguments advanced by Churchland to justify the elimination (rather than smooth reduction) of folk psychology:\n1. *The Argument from Widespread Explanatory Failure*: Folk psychology leaves the vast majority of mental phenomena completely unexplained—including the nature of sleep, dreams, memory consolidation, mental illness, learning mechanics, and perceptual illusions.\n2. *The Argument from Historical Stagnation*: Folk psychology has not made any significant theoretical progress in 2,500 years; Aristotle or Sophocles understood human behavior using the exact same propositional attitude framework we use today.\n3. *The Argument from Historical Precedent*: Just as past false theories were not smoothly reduced but outright eliminated—such as the phlogiston theory of combustion (replaced by oxygen chemistry), the caloric theory of heat (replaced by thermodynamics), and the witch theory of psychosis (replaced by neuropathology)—so propositional attitudes will be eliminated by mature cognitive neuroscience.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are genuine tenets of Churchland’s argument for Eliminative Materialism?",
      "options": {
        "A": "1, 2, and 3",
        "B": "1 and 2 only",
        "C": "2 and 3 only",
        "D": "1 only"
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "Paul Churchland’s 1981 paper is the definitive statement of **Eliminative Materialism**. Churchland defines eliminativism as:\n> \"The thesis that our common-sense conception of psychological phenomena constitutes a radically false theory, a theory so fundamentally defective that both the principles and the ontology of that theory will eventually be displaced, rather than smoothly reduced, by completed neuroscience.\"\n\nHe structures his case around three foundational arguments:\n1. *Explanatory Poverty (Statement 1)*: Folk psychology is remarkably narrow. It cannot explain the physiological necessity of sleep, the mechanism of dreaming, the retrieval of memories, the etiology of schizophrenia, or developmental differences in learning.\n2. *Theoretical Stagnation (Statement 2)*: A healthy scientific research programme expands its predictive domain and develops new explanatory laws. Folk psychology, by contrast, is stagnant: the explanation of human action in Homer or Sophocles in terms of beliefs, passions, and desires is essentially identical to our common-sense discourse today. It is a degenerating research programme (in Imre Lakatos’s sense).\n3. *Coherence with Physical Science & Historical Precedent (Statement 3)*: When a theoretical ontology cannot be matched to the categories of the underlying science, it is *eliminated*, not reduced:\n   - *Phlogiston* was not reduced to oxygen; scientists realized there is no such entity as phlogiston.\n   - *Caloric fluid* was eliminated once heat was recognized as molecular kinetic energy.\n   - *Witches* were not reduced to neurotics; the category was discarded.\nSimilarly, Churchland argues that beliefs and desires will not map neatly onto neural networks; they will be eliminated as obsolete folk fictions.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B), (C), and (D) are incorrect**: All three arguments (Explanatory Deficits, Stagnation, and Historical Elimination) are canonical, foundational pillars of Churchland's 1981 thesis.",
      "reference": "Paul M. Churchland, \"Eliminative Materialism and the Propositional Attitudes\", *Journal of Philosophy* 78(2) (1981): 67–90."
