      "grounding": "When sensory information is flashed to the left visual field (routed to the right hemisphere), a split-brain patient cannot verbally name the object (since speech is localized in the left hemisphere), but their left hand (controlled by the right hemisphere) can effortlessly reach behind a screen, locate the object by touch, and draw it. \n\nIn \"Brain Bisection and the Unity of Consciousness\" (1971), Thomas Nagel analyzes the five conceivable interpretations of this phenomenon:\n1. *Hypothesis 1*: The patient has a single, normal unified mind, but disconnected pathways. (Refuted because the two hemispheres can simultaneously perform conflicting voluntary intentional tasks).\n2. *Hypothesis 2*: The patient has two completely independent conscious minds—a speaking left-mind and a mute right-mind. (Refuted because outside the laboratory, in ordinary daily life, the patient exhibits integrated, unified, non-conflicting personal behavior).\n3. *Hypothesis 3*: The patient has one conscious mind (the left hemisphere) and an unconscious automaton (the right hemisphere). (Refuted because the right hemisphere exhibits learning, emotion, intention, and perceptual discrimination).\n4. *Hypothesis 4*: The patient has one normal mind that switches back and forth. (Refuted by simultaneous dual-task execution).\n5. *Hypothesis 5*: The mind is a combination of 1.5 minds. (Lacks any coherent semantic meaning).\n\nNagel concludes that **the ordinary common-sense concept of a single, indivisible conscious subject breaks down**:\n> \"The idea of a single subject of experience cannot be applied to the split-brain patient. But neither can the idea of two subjects. The radical conclusion is that the concept of an individual subject of consciousness is an illusion that cannot withstand physical dissection of the anatomical substrate.\"",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Nagel rejects Cartesian dualism; Descartes claimed the soul is indivisible (*res cogitans* has no parts), which commissurotomy directly refutes.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: Gazzaniga and Sperry proved that the mute right hemisphere is undeniably conscious, possesses emotional reactions, and comprehends complex language.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: Nagel’s conclusion is physicalist, skeptical, and bundle-theoretic; he does not postulate metaphysical immortal souls.",
