      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "The Predictive Processing (PP) framework represents a profound paradigm shift in cognitive science:\n1. *Classical Bottom-Up View*: The sensory organs receive physical energy (photons, sound waves) and transmit raw data upward through a hierarchical chain of feature detectors (V1 edges $\\to$ V2 textures $\\to$ V4 shapes $\\to$ IT objects), passively building an internal representation of the world.\n2. *The Predictive Inversion*: In PP, the brain is an **inference engine** that constantly minimizes variational free energy (an information-theoretic upper bound on surprise):\n   - **Top-Down Generative Models**: Higher cortical areas continuously project predictive models down to lower sensory areas, anticipating what sensory signals *should* be occurring based on prior knowledge ($P(\\text{Hypothesis})$).\n   - **Prediction Error Minimization**: Lower areas compare the actual sensory input with the top-down prediction. Crucially, the sensory data that matches the prediction is suppressed and filtered out! What travels *up* the cortical hierarchy is **only the prediction error**:\n     $$\\text{Prediction Error} = \\text{Actual Input} - \\text{Top-Down Prediction}$$\n   - **Perception as Controlled Hallucination**: What we consciously perceive is not the raw bottom-up sensory input; we consciously experience our own **best, error-minimized top-down generative hypothesis**. Perception is, in Anil Seth's and Andy Clark's vivid phrase, \"controlled hallucination\"—hallucination that is tightly anchored and disciplined by residual sensory prediction errors.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: This is the naive direct realist / bottom-up view that predictive processing explicitly refutes; transmitting raw, uncompressed sensory data would be computationally inefficient and biologically impossible.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: Predictive processing is not radical solipsism; the prediction error signal ensures that the internal generative model is constantly disciplined, updated, and corrected by worldly sensory reality.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: In predictive coding, sensory cortices are deeply recurrent and bidirectionally coupled; top-down signals dominate sensory processing, not just motor actions.",
      "reference": "Andy Clark, \"Whatever Next? Predictive Brains, Situated Agents, and the Future of Cognitive Science\", *Behavioral and Brain Sciences* 36(3) (2013): 181–204; and Jakob Hohwy, *The Predictive Mind* (Oxford University Press, 2013), Chapter 1: \"The Prediction Error Minimization Mechanism\", pp. 13–40."
