      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "In the predictive processing hierarchy, prediction errors and prior predictions are constantly balanced through **precision weighting** ($\\Pi$):\n- $\\Pi$ acts as a gain control (analogous to turning up the volume on a reliable microphone or turning it down on a noisy channel).\n- If sensory inputs are deemed noisy or unreliable (e.g., in a dark, foggy forest), the brain turns *down* sensory precision and leans heavily on prior expectations.\n- If priors are weak and sensory input is pristine (e.g., broad daylight), sensory precision is high, forcing the generative model to update immediately.\n\nIn neurophilosophical psychopathology (Friston, Corlett, Fletcher, Hohwy):\n- **Hallucinations** occur when the brain pathologically **over-weights the precision of top-down priors** relative to bottom-up sensory prediction errors. \n- Even when the incoming sensory data flatly contradicts the expectation (e.g., there is no voice speaking in the empty room), the prediction error is disregarded as \"noisy\" due to low precision weighting.\n- The overwhelming top-down prior prediction settles the internal generative state without being corrected by sensory reality. Because conscious experience reflects the settled generative model, the subject genuinely, phenomenologically hears a voice!\n- **Delusions** (like Capgras delusion or persecutory beliefs) represent the higher-order cognitive attempt of the brain to invent a bizarre narrative that minimizes prediction errors that lower levels have failed to explain away.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: In psychiatric auditory or visual hallucinations, the peripheral sensory organs (retina, cochlea) are typically fully functional; the pathology is localized in central cortical predictive weighting.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: This is pseudoscientific nonsense; neurons do not fire backward in time.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: Patients with schizophrenia or hallucinations retain complex, highly articulate conceptual frameworks (often excessively hyper-rationalizing their delusions).",
      "reference": "Philip R. Corlett et al., \"Hallucinations and Perceptual Inference\", *Neuron* 102(2) (2019): 300–310; Karl Friston, \"The Free-Energy Principle: A Unified Brain Theory?\", *Nature Reviews Neuroscience* 11(2) (2010): 127–138."
