      "is_mock": false,
      "domain": "Philosophy of AI",
      "tier": "Tier 4: Conceptually Hard / Frontiers",
      "title": "Whole Brain Emulation, Digital Minds, and Derek Parfit's Fission Paradox",
      "thinkers": "Derek Parfit (*Reasons and Persons*, 1984), Nick Bostrom & Anders Sandberg (2008), David Chalmers (2010)",
      "question": "Suppose an artificial intelligence architecture succeeds in achieving **Whole Brain Emulation (WBE)** via non-destructive molecular-level scanning and digital reconstruction of a human mind. Upon digital activation, two identical digital emulations (**Emulator Alpha** and **Emulator Beta**) are launched simultaneously on separate supercomputing clusters. How does Derek Parfit's psychological reductionism (*Bundle Theory of Self*) resolve the question of personal identity across this digital copying event?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Personal identity strictly branches: neither Alpha nor Beta is identical to the biological original in the 1-to-1 metaphysical sense of identity ($x = y$), yet Relation $R$ (psychological connectedness and continuity) is fully preserved between the original and both emulations, demonstrating that personal identity is not what fundamentally matters for survival.",
        "B": "Emulator Alpha inherits the Cartesian immaterial soul, while Emulator Beta becomes an unfeeling philosophical zombie with zero subjective experience.",
        "C": "Both emulations instantly collapse into a single unified conscious awareness through quantum mechanical entanglement across the two server racks.",
        "D": "The original person survives exclusively in Emulator Alpha because the operating system gave Alpha priority in CPU scheduling threads."
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "In *Reasons and Persons* (1984), Derek Parfit introduced the profound distinction between:\n- **Strict Identity**: A transitive, symmetrical, one-to-one relation ($A = B$). Identity cannot branch: if $A$ divides into $B$ and $C$, $A$ cannot be identical to both $B$ and $C$ without violating the transitivity of identity ($B = C$, which is false, since $B$ and $C$ lead separate lives).\n- **Relation $R$**: Psychological continuity and/or psychological connectedness, with the right kind of cause.\n\nIn the case of digital brain emulation or digital mind uploading:\n1. If non-destructive scanning creates two running digital instances (Alpha and Beta), standard metaphysical views of personal identity face an insoluble trilemma:\n   - Does the original die? (Absurd, since survival would have occurred if only one copy had been run).\n   - Does the original become Alpha? (Arbitrary asymmetry, since Beta is chemically and computationally identical).\n   - Does the original become both? (Violates transitivity: Alpha and Beta can make conflicting moral choices, move to different planets, and die at different times).\n2. Parfit's revolutionary solution is that **personal identity is not what matters**. What matters in ordinary survival is **Relation $R$**.\n3. In digital mind duplication/fission, Relation $R$ holds fully between the biological original and Alpha, and between the biological original and Beta. The original person survives twice over! Fission is not death; it is as good as ordinary survival, even though strict numerical identity has branched.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Parfit rejects the Cartesian Ego / Non-Reductionist \"Simple View\" of the soul; physicalism and computational functionalism maintain that both instances have identical functional architectures and subjective experiences.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: This commits the fallacy of quantum mysticism; separate digital processes running on classical hardware remain distinct computational streams.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: Operating system CPU scheduling priority or process IDs are accidental technical variables that cannot ground metaphysical personal identity.",
      "reference": "Derek Parfit, *Reasons and Persons* (Oxford University Press, 1984), Part III (\"Personal Identity\"), esp. Chapter 12 (\"Why Our Identity Is Not What Matters\"); Anders Sandberg and Nick Bostrom, *Whole Brain Emulation: A Roadmap*, Technical Report #2008-3, Future of Humanity Institute, Oxford University (2008)."
